From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Salah Ahmed <haddad2inc@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 23:45:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] leds: trigger-netdev: add byte-rate threshold mode

leds: trigger-netdev: add byte-rate threshold mode

The tx/rx modes blink once per observed change of the packet counters,
which misses traffic forwarded in hardware (DSA bridge offload) and
flickers on background broadcast noise. Add a rate_threshold attribute
(KiB/s): when non-zero the trigger drives the LED from the interface byte
rate instead, blinking on a 50..150 ms ladder above the threshold and
resting in the baseline state (solid on link, dark without carrier) below
it.

The rate is measured over the interval between two observed counter
changes, not over a fixed window. Drivers that refresh their counters in
bursts - a cached switch MIB polled every few seconds, for instance -
would otherwise dump a whole accumulation period into the first fixed
window that follows the refresh: the reported rate is then several times
the real one, followed by zero until the next refresh, which turns the LED
into a stutter instead of a steady blink. Measuring between changes makes
the trigger independent of the driver's refresh interval, and the last
measured rate is kept until the counter has been silent for twice the
interval it was measured over (at least one poll period, capped at 10 s)
so that the blink lasts as long as the traffic does.

The blink is re-armed only when the ladder step changes: led_blink_set()
restarts the software blink timer and truncates the phase in progress, so
re-arming it on every evaluation shows up as an irregular cadence.

Rate mode is independent of the link/tx/rx mode bits on purpose, so that
ports whose packet counters are blind to offloaded flows still show
activity. It is evaluated in software, so can_hw_control() refuses it the
way it already refuses a non-default interval, and writing the attribute
re-decides hw control: a non-zero threshold takes the LED back from the
driver, zero may hand it over again. Writing 0 also restores the link
state for an LED with no tx/rx bit set, because the packet path only
touches the LED when the activity counter changes.

Byte counters can legitimately go backwards when a driver resets them, and
an unsigned subtraction would then report an enormous rate, so a
decreasing counter re-anchors the measurement instead. Two counter changes
inside one tick would leave the interval at zero milliseconds, so the
divisor is floored, and div_u64() keeps 32-bit architectures linking
without __udivdi3/__aeabi_uldivmod.

Bursty transfers can straddle a ladder boundary on every evaluation,
and cutting the blink off the instant the counter goes quiet makes a
pausing transfer look like the LED died. The measured rate is therefore
smoothed the way field-accepted userspace activity daemons do it -
immediate attack at or above the threshold, a slow rise and a quick
release below it - and when the counter goes silent the blink fades out
quadratically from the last peak over 20 s. The decay is monotone, so the
ladder step lengthens on its own while the fade lasts and needs no clamp
against the step that was armed before it.

set_baseline_state() runs after every netdev notifier event and after
every mode or device_name write, and both callers cancel the work first.
It sets a solid brightness and cancels the work first, so with a rate
threshold set it also owns the rate-mode bookkeeping: stop a software
blink that is still armed - before the early return that hands the LED to
hardware, which would otherwise leave it blinking - clear the armed ladder
step, drop the measurement when there is no carrier, and reschedule the
work. Rescheduling is what keeps rate mode alive on a link-only LED across
carrier changes and honest on tx/rx LEDs under load: without it the work
computes the same ladder step as before, skips the re-arm while the
brightness has just been reset to solid, and a below-threshold sample after
a cleared rate_last_ms leaves an old blink running. The work itself stops
polling while there is no carrier, because nothing is measurable then, and
the next NETDEV_UP or carrier-returning NETDEV_CHANGE re-arms it.

The state a rate-mode blink rests in is the trigger's baseline state, not
a second policy: netdev_baseline_on() answers "is this LED on at rest?"
from the same link, speed and duplex mode bits the baseline uses, and
netdev_set_resting() stops a blink that is still armed and then applies
the same level, blink_brightness. Both set_baseline_state() and the
post-blink path call it, so the two cannot drift apart: a mode without a
link bit rests dark, and a multi-level LED rests at its blinking level
rather than at LED_ON (1), which would read nearly dark. On a single-level
LED (max_brightness = 1) the two values coincide, so nothing differs
there.

rate_threshold_store() cancels the work and then takes the mutex the
notifier and set_device_name() hold while they change the same fields, so
a store cannot land between a netdev event and the baseline update that
follows it and leave the threshold and the measurement describing two
different interfaces.

Signed-off-by: Salah Ahmed <haddad2inc@gmail.com>
